WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:07.730
Miriam: I am a child survivor from Poland.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:15.400
Poland was home to the largest Jewish population
in Europe.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:26.280
By inviting a Holocaust survivor, you remember
the murder of Jews of Europe.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.940
You keep their memory alive.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.340
Ayla: Miriam’s story to me is very touching.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.620
You know, being Jewish, I have family who
were lost in the Holocaust, and we have no

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.390
records whatsoever, in many cases not even
a name, and it’s very touching to see Miriam’s

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.710
story and how she brought her memories together.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.620
Abby: It’s inspiring, it’s heartwarming,
it’s moving.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.629
I think it’s brilliant that she was able
to write this story out and go through all

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:11.940
of that again, relive it.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:31.690
Abby: So Miriam, your book is all about, you
know, rediscovering your memory and then sharing

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:38.000
those memories in your story, and I was wondering
what specifically motivated you to rediscover

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.880
your Jewish heritage?

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:48.840
Miriam: I don’t even think that we can speak
about “rediscovering,” because I was eight,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.580
over eight.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:59.750
I was born June 2nd, I left home in ’41
in November, so it’s eight and three months.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.020
I knew who I was.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:09.690
I was hiding, actively hiding.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:26.489
So when I returned to who I was, it was a
very slow, fearful process.

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:29.370
I was really afraid.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:35.079
Ayla: So in Trains, you wrote a lot about
the nature of perspective, and how you remember

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:37.030
differently now that you’re older.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.879
As you were writing Trains, how did your perspective
on your story change.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:52.329
Miriam: The story is the same, but I allowed
myself, as I went along with the book, I allowed

00:02:52.329 --> 00:03:00.810
myself to admit to feelings, to admit to hurt.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:09.180
I think it helped me become a truer person.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:19.629
When I decided to tell my story, and that
was after the first gathering of child survivors,

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:26.299
and I stood up, was out waiting for my turn
to run through the middle of the room, and

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:33.749
started talking, just words poured out of
me, and I think some recollection of things

00:03:33.749 --> 00:03:41.699
that were there, but I did not recall them
really, did not acknowledge them – like

00:03:41.699 --> 00:03:49.510
my grandfather praying, he was a Hassidic
Jew – and I was speaking about that.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:59.150
And when I sat down, also by the reaction
of people, the survivors, it was a gathering

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:10.339
of the survivors, I realized that I need to
write.

00:04:10.339 --> 00:04:21.970
So when I started writing, I looked at a photograph
of me, the young Winter at her first communion,

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:30.210
that’s the only thing I had, nothing physical
except for that photograph.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:44.389
And it really went like a spiral, deeper and
deeper, when I allowed myself to not rush,

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:54.870
but also to stop in the middle of a sentence,
or pick up from nowhere and just start writing.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:05:03.340
Ben: There’s a part of your book where you
say, “I am their witness,” and the theme

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.370
of witnessing comes up a lot in your book.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.530
What is your goal as a witness?

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:17.639
Miriam: To tell what I know happened.

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:26.740
My field is theater, but my hobby through
all my life was history, and I do know history.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:37.500
But I do not, when I speak about the Holocaust,
I speak only about the things that I know,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:49.240
and that’s important for me because I want
to tell what had happened from that perspective,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:06:00.580
from what as a child, during the Holocaust,
in Poland, what I witnessed – not what I

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:04.840
heard, what I witnessed of what had happened.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:10.490
Ayla: So what do you hope that students especially
will learn as they read your story?

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:21.170
Miriam: The story must be told, and the memory
of what happened must be kept alive.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:31.060
That was, that is now, a very strong motivation
for many other Jewish child survivors, not

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.159
only for me.

00:06:32.159 --> 00:07:26.400
It definitely is for me, and I think it was
so from the very beginning: to tell what happened

